  "open": [
    {
      "id": "GF-0098",
      "title": "--http2 runs an h2c Upgrade in Curl (exit 8), while the Windows reference curl, built without nghttp2, refuses --http2 with exit 2",
      "area": "behaviour",
      "platform": "windows",
      "severity": "High",
      "scope": "target",
      "introducedIn": null,
      "suggestion": "No change to Curl's HTTP/2 Upgrade handling: Curl.Protocol.Http.UnitLibrary already does what upstream's test1704 <verify> asks (exit 8, the h2c request bytes), and the gap comes only from the Windows reference build having no HTTP2/h2c feature. Recommend rejecting the finding: to match it literally, Curl.Cli.UnitLibrary (CommandLineParser.cs / CommandLineRefusal.cs) would have to refuse --http2 on Windows with exit 2 and 'option --http2: the installed libcurl version does not support this', which removes a feature and breaks the 'complete reimplementation is the default answer' rule in CLAUDE.md. The lasting fix is office work: the measurement should exclude a case the harness ran as reference-lacks:<feature> when its <features> names one the reference's curl -V lacks (h2c here). Today rule 4 of Gap/Tools/ConvertTo-BehaviourMeasurement.ps1 checks this only for cases the harness skipped (Get-SkippedVerdict). Explained by ADR-0788: an HTTP/2 status line over HTTP/1 is refused with exit 8 on every platform, measured from an HTTP/2-built Windows curl.",
      "items": 1,
      "tasks": [
        "BL-3431"
      ],
      "url": "https://github.com/StewartScottRogers/Curl/blob/gap/Gap/Findings/GF-0098-http2-runs-an-h2c-upgrade-in-curl-exit-8-while-the-windows-r.md"
    },
    {
      "id": "GF-0031",
      "title": "Curl lists HTTP2 in Features; the Windows reference build does not",
      "area": "features",
      "platform": "windows",
      "severity": "Medium",
      "scope": "target",
      "introducedIn": null,
      "suggestion": "In Curl.Cli.UnitLibrary/CurlVersionText.cs, make FeaturesLine platform-specific and drop HTTP2 from the Windows line, keeping it where the matched reference lists it. Update CurlVersionTextTests in Curl.Cli.UnitTests. Explained by ADR-0141: HTTP/2 is hand-built and accepted everywhere, but offered by default only off Windows, so the Windows build does not offer it by default.",
      "items": 1,
      "tasks": [
        "BL-1824",
        "BL-3430"
      ],
      "url": "https://github.com/StewartScottRogers/Curl/blob/gap/Gap/Findings/GF-0031-curl-lists-http2-in-features-the-windows-reference-build-doe.md"
    },
    {
      "id": "GF-0099",
      "title": "HTTP/2 options are accepted by Curl but refused by the reference curl as unsupported by its libcurl",
      "area": "options",
      "platform": "windows",
      "severity": "Medium",
      "scope": "target",
      "introducedIn": "7.33.0",
      "suggestion": "The Windows reference curl (Git for Windows, Schannel) was built without HTTP/2, so it refuses these options. Curl implements HTTP/2 and accepts them. The gap is a reference-build difference, not a missing option: the yardstick is upstream's release, where a build with nghttp2 accepts these options. Close it by one of two routes. (a) Re-measure these facets against a reference curl built with HTTP/2, and mark them match or excluded with a reason. (b) Add a build-feature switch in Curl.Cli.UnitLibrary (the CommandLineParser branch that handles --http2, --http2-prior-knowledge and --proxy-http2, with their entries in CommandLineOptionTable) so a Curl build without HTTP/2 prints 'option --X: the installed libcurl version does not support this' (see docs/cmdline-opts/http2.md, http2-prior-knowledge.md and proxy-http2.md). Route (a) is likely right, because Curl always carries HTTP/2. Keep `--ai-help` right for the options this changes (CLAUDE.md).",
      "items": 4,
      "tasks": [
        "BL-3432"
      ],
      "url": "https://github.com/StewartScottRogers/Curl/blob/gap/Gap/Findings/GF-0099-http-2-options-are-accepted-by-curl-but-refused-by-the-refer.md"
    }
  ],
  "closed": [
    {
      "id": "GF-0097",
      "title": "An empty SSL_CERT_DIR (or CA path) crashes Curl with an unhandled ArgumentException (exit 134) as soon as a TLS transfer starts",
      "area": "environment",
      "platform": "linux",
      "severity": "Critical",
      "scope": "target",
      "introducedIn": null,
      "suggestion": "Most likely cause: CaCertificateEnvironment.Apply (Curl.Cli.UnitLibrary) copies the SSL_CERT_DIR value verbatim, and the measuring tool most likely clears the variable by setting it empty on Linux. The empty value reaches ServerCertificateVerification.ReadCaCertificateDirectory (Curl.Networking.UnitLibrary) and Directory.GetFiles(\"\") throws ArgumentException, which ListFilesOrNothing does not catch. Fix: (1) in ListFilesOrNothing and ReadTextOrNothing catch ArgumentException and NotSupportedException too, so an empty or malformed --capath adds nothing, as in curl's OpenSSL build. (2) In ReadCaCertificates, treat an empty CaCertificateFile as an unusable file (exit 77) and never let an unhandled exception escape. An empty SSL_CERT_FILE would otherwise crash in File.ReadAllText(\"\"). (3) Confirm with a probe what curl 8.22.0 does with SSL_CERT_DIR='' and SSL_CERT_FILE='' and match it (docs/cmdline-opts/_ENVIRONMENT.md, CURL_CA_BUNDLE / SSL_CERT_DIR / SSL_CERT_FILE). Add focused unit tests in Curl.Networking.UnitTests and Curl.Cli.UnitTests. This is a linux/macos cause (the Schannel build reads none of these variables).",
      "items": 3,
      "tasks": [
        "BL-2822"
      ],
      "url": "https://github.com/StewartScottRogers/Curl/blob/gap/Gap/Findings/GF-0097-an-empty-ssl-cert-dir-or-ca-path-crashes-curl-with-an-unhand.md"
    },
    {
      "id": "GF-0083",
      "title": "An 'HTTP/2 101' answer to an h2c upgrade request followed by an HTTP/1.1 200 exits 0 instead of 8",
      "area": "behaviour",
      "platform": "linux",
      "severity": "High",
      "scope": "target",
      "introducedIn": null,
      "suggestion": "In Curl.Protocol.Http.UnitLibrary, fail with exit 8 (CURLE_WEIRD_SERVER_REPLY) when a response on an HTTP/1.x connection names HTTP/2 in its status line, including the interim response to an h2c upgrade. Today HttpH2cUpgradeConnection switches only on 'HTTP/1.1 101'. Otherwise it reads on as HTTP/1.1, and HttpStatusLine.Parse takes 'HTTP/2 101' as an assumed interim answer before accepting the 200. Have the 1xx head record its version (Http1VersionSeen.VersionNamed) so that Http1VersionSeen.ThrowIfMismatched, or a direct check, refuses the 'HTTP/2' status line as curl does. Add a focused test in Curl.Protocol.Http.UnitTests (ADR-0482). This applies only on the OpenSSL builds, because the Schannel reference lacks h2c.",
      "items": 1,
      "tasks": [
        "BL-2326",
        "BL-2715"
      ],
      "url": "https://github.com/StewartScottRogers/Curl/blob/gap/Gap/Findings/GF-0083-an-http-2-101-answer-to-an-h2c-upgrade-request-followed-by-a.md"
    },
    {
      "id": "GF-0084",
      "title": "SSLKEYLOGFILE is ignored: no CLIENT_RANDOM key-log line is written for a TLS connection",
      "area": "behaviour",
      "platform": "linux",
      "severity": "High",
      "scope": "target",
      "introducedIn": null,
      "suggestion": "Implement the OpenSSL build's SSLKEYLOGFILE support. When the variable names a file, append NSS key-log lines to it. For TLS 1.2 that is 'CLIENT_RANDOM <client_random hex> <master_secret hex>'. For TLS 1.3 it is the CLIENT_HANDSHAKE_TRAFFIC_SECRET, SERVER_HANDSHAKE_TRAFFIC_SECRET, CLIENT_TRAFFIC_SECRET_0, SERVER_TRAFFIC_SECRET_0 and EXPORTER_SECRET lines. Write them from the hand-built clients in Curl.Tls.UnitLibrary (Tls12ClientConnection, which --tls-max 1.2 routes to through HandBuiltTlsProvider, plus Tls13ClientConnection and TlsClientConnection). Carry the path in through TlsClientOptions, read from the environment in Curl.Engine.UnitLibrary (TlsClientOptionsMapping). Make sure the SslStream path (SslStreamTlsProvider) logs too, or routes to the hand-built client, when the variable is set. Upstream's Schannel build does not support it (test2090 requires !Schannel), so leave the Windows build alone. Add unit tests in Curl.Tls.UnitTests.",
      "items": 1,
      "tasks": [
        "BL-2327",
        "BL-2716"
      ],
      "url": "https://github.com/StewartScottRogers/Curl/blob/gap/Gap/Findings/GF-0084-sslkeylogfile-is-ignored-no-client-random-key-log-line-is-wr.md"
    },
    {
      "id": "GF-0094",
      "title": "HTTP/3 cases run on Linux although Curl, like the OpenSSL reference curl, refuses --http3 and --http3-only there",
      "area": "behaviour",
      "platform": "linux",
      "severity": "High",
      "scope": "target",
      "introducedIn": null,
      "suggestion": "In Curl.Conformance.UnitLibrary, remove 'http/3' from UpstreamCurlPlatform.Unix and UpstreamCurlPlatform.MacOS. Fix the class remarks that say Curl still serves --http3 off Windows. Upstream's runtests.pl then skips cases needing http/3 on a build without QUIC, as it does for the reference, and they are excluded as on Windows. As an alternative, derive the feature from CommandLineOptions.ActsAsQuicBuild so it follows the CLI's refusal. Update UpstreamCurlPlatformTests. The product needs no change: Curl already matches the reference here. Explained by ADR-0540 (refuse --http3 on every reference build); the harness's feature list predates it (BL-2261).",
      "items": 2,
      "tasks": [
        "BL-2370"
      ],
      "url": "https://github.com/StewartScottRogers/Curl/blob/gap/Gap/Findings/GF-0094-http-3-cases-run-on-linux-although-curl-like-the-openssl-ref.md"
    },
    {
      "id": "GF-0095",
      "title": "A chunked -T - upload that waited for 100 Continue sends its closing chunk in a separate write, so Curl exits 56 on macOS where curl exits 0",
      "area": "behaviour",
      "platform": "macos",
      "severity": "High",
      "scope": "target",
      "introducedIn": null,
      "suggestion": "In Curl.Protocol.Http.UnitLibrary, HttpRequestBodyWriter.StartReading sets heldBack only when EarlyResponseWatch is null, so a chunked stream body of unknown length that waited for 100 Continue (test60: curl adds Expect: 100-continue to the -T - upload, the 1-second wait runs out) still writes its data chunk through HttpContinueWaitConnection.SendUnlessStoppedAsync and then 0\\r\\n\\r\\n in a write of its own. The sws-like server reads the one byte Content-Length promises after the first write, answers and closes; the closing chunk then meets a closed socket and macOS resets the connection, so the response read fails with exit 56. Extend the BL-2341 hold-back to the early-response path: hold each framed piece until the next read and send the last data chunk and the closing chunk in one SendUnlessStoppedAsync call (keeping the 300-or-above stop and CutShort), as curl fills its upload buffer to end of stream before it sends. Add a focused unit test in Curl.Protocol.Http.UnitTests with a fake IConnection that records writes: an Expect: 100-continue chunked upload of unknown length whose wait runs out gives one write holding '13\\r\\nmore than one byte\\n\\r\\n0\\r\\n\\r\\n' (ADR-0482). Explained by ADR-0525: its decision covers only bodies 'sent without waiting for 100 Continue' and leaves 'bodies sent after 100 Continue (whose early-response watch sends piece by piece)' unchanged, which is exactly test60's path; the macOS exit 56 the ADR's context names is therefore still open. The reset-on-late-write is a BSD socket behaviour, which is why only macOS shows it.",
      "items": 1,
      "tasks": [
        "BL-2371"
      ],
      "url": "https://github.com/StewartScottRogers/Curl/blob/gap/Gap/Findings/GF-0095-a-chunked-t-upload-that-waited-for-100-continue-sends-its-cl.md"
    },
